the watchdog starts
!panic: this runtime's allocator was entered again before a call into it had finished, which would corrupt memory: either a signal handler used it while the code it interrupted was inside it (a Heroes function given to C as a signal handler, or called from one, does so whenever it builds or drops a value), or a signal handler jumped (`siglongjmp`, `longjmp`) out of the code it interrupted while that code was inside it, which nothing will ever finish. No function of the program is a signal handler: after `hero_net_catch_stop() -> i64` (`hero_net.h`), Ctrl-C, SIGTERM and `hero_net_ask_stop() -> ()` only make `hero_net_stop_asked() -> i64` answer 1 and a waiting call of that layer, or a mailbox's `receive` that finds it empty, answer stopped; `hero_signal_watch(sig: i32) -> i64` (`hero_os.h`) counts any other signal, and `hero_signal_take(sig: i32) -> i64` gives the count since its last call
